Event Logs

To find server reboot logs for the last three months, follow these steps:
	1. Open the Windows Event Viewer by pressing Windows Key + R and typing eventvwr.msc.
	2. Navigate to Windows Logs > System.
	3. Use the Filter Current Log option to filter by Event ID 6005 (system startup), 6006 (system shutdown), and 41 (unexpected reboot).
	4. Check the logs for any events that indicate a reboot, such as Event ID 1074, which records the user or process that triggered the restart.
	• 1074: Initiated a shutdown or restart. It shows which user initiated it and the reason (e.g., updates, application).
	• 6008: The previous system shutdown was unexpected, typically logged after power loss or a crash.
	• 41 (Kernel-Power): System rebooted without a clean shutdown; usually indicates a critical crash or power supply issue.
	• 6005: Logs when the Event Log service starts, indicating the system booted up.
	• 6006: Logs that the system shut down cleanly.
	• 1076: Records the reason for an unexpected shutdown when a user with privileges logs in
	
	5. Review the logs for any events that indicate an unexpected reboot, such as Event ID 6008, which logs the system shutting down abnormally.

Get Active Events Categories
Get-WinEvent -ListLog * |
    Select-Object LogName, RecordCount, IsEnabled

Search for text inside event messages
Get-WinEvent -LogName System |
    Where-Object {
        $_.Message -like "*disk*"
    } |
    Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, ProviderName, Message

If you don't know which log — search ALL logs (Windows Logs, Application & Service Logs)

$EventID = 10016

Get-WinEvent -ListLog * -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
ForEach-Object {
    try {
        Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{
            LogName = $_.LogName
            Id      = $EventID
        } -MaxEvents 5 -ErrorAction Stop
    }
    catch {
        # Ignore inaccessible logs
    }
} |
Select-Object TimeCreated,
              LogName,
              Id,
              LevelDisplayName,
              ProviderName,
              MachineName,
              RecordId,
              Message |
Format-List

A faster version for the three major logs

$EventID = 10016

'System','Application','Security' | ForEach-Object {
    Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{
        LogName = $_
        Id      = $EventID
    } -MaxEvents 10 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
} |
Select-Object TimeCreated,
              LogName,
              Id,
              LevelDisplayName,
              ProviderName,
              MachineName,
              RecordId,
              Message |
Format-List
